  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: c53f4bc5042955a41b24ca7531dc6614, type: 3}
  m_Name: Cemetery Letter
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CinematicSequenceSO
  slides:
  - lines:
    - Father, I do not know why I still write to you after all these years. Maybe
      because there is no one left who remembers me as I was before.
    - I still remember the smell of your workshop and the sound of people talking
      outside our window before Shabbat. I remember how tired you looked during your
      last winter, even when you tried not to show it to us. Even with the war growing
      closer, none of us truly believed that our whole world could disappear so quickly.
    - After you died, Mama sent me to stay with the family of Petro, the man who
      worked with you for many years. I remember how often he came to the workshop
      and stayed late talking with you about work, deliveries, and people in nearby
      towns. Mama thought it would be good for me to earn some money, help his family
      with sewing and housework, and spend some time away after everything that happened.
    autoAdvance: 0
    autoAdvanceDelay: 0
    lineFadeInDuration: 0
    delayBetweenLines: 0
    triggerCharacterSelection: 0
    triggerCustomEvent: 0
    customEventId: 
  - lines:
    - I was supposed to return in a few weeks. At first everyone believed the war
      would pass quickly. People argued about rumors, listened for news, and tried
      to continue living normally. No one imagined what was coming for the Jews.
    - "When the occupation began, Petro\u2019s family hid me in their house. During
      searches I stayed in the cellar or the barn. They gave me another name, and
      after some time I began answering to it without thinking."
    - I still do not understand why I survived while the others did not. Everything
      depended on one journey that was never meant to save me.
    - "Sometimes, late in the evening, Petro\u2019s children asked me to sing songs
      from home. I tried to remember the words, but after a while even my own voice
      began to sound unfamiliar to me."
    autoAdvance: 0
    autoAdvanceDelay: 0
    lineFadeInDuration: 0
    delayBetweenLines: 0
    triggerCharacterSelection: 0
    triggerCustomEvent: 0
    customEventId: 
  - lines:
    - When the war ended, I could not make myself return to the town. I told myself
      there was nothing left there anymore. But the truth is that I was afraid.
    - Afraid to see the empty streets. Afraid to stand beside your grave and realize
      how many years had passed without me there. Afraid that if I returned, all
      of it would finally become real.
    - So I buried everything instead. I stopped speaking about home. I locked the
      photographs, letters, and documents away in a box and built another life around
      the silence. Even my daughter knew almost nothing about where I came from.
      I told myself I was protecting her from grief, but perhaps I was only protecting
      myself from memory...
    - Please forgive me for never coming back. Your daughter, Feiga.
    autoAdvance: 0
    autoAdvanceDelay: 0
    lineFadeInDuration: 0
    delayBetweenLines: 0
    triggerCharacterSelection: 0
    triggerCustomEvent: 0
    customEventId: 
